<h1>Video 080 — Exception Handling compilation</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Java Exception Handling For  Certification &amp; Interviews || Java Exception Handling || by Durga Sir</p>
<h2>Important: this is a mega compilation, not a new lecture</h2>
<p>This upload is a ~10-hour re-package of the individual Exception Handling lectures already in the same playlist (videos 070–079). It does not replace those component lectures with new certification content.</p>
<p>Captions: YouTube has no usable auto-captions for this mega upload (none that can drive timestamped board-level notes). Do not treat this file as a full transcript of the 10h video.</p>
<p>Where the real notes live: Detailed caption-based lecture notes with Java board examples are in the component videos 070–079 under notes/chunk-061-080/. Study those files (and the shorter part videos) for board timestamps, ASR-decoded board text, and full examples.</p>
<p>This file is only an index + OCJP study checklist + exam-checklist snippets for the compilation entry at position 80.</p>
<h2>Mapping: compilation topics → individual playlist videos</h2>
<p>Watch URLs for component videos: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=&lt;VideoID&gt; using IDs from notes/playlist.tsv (rows 070–079).</p>
<h2>OCJP study checklist (topics covered by this compilation)</h2>
<p>Use this as a revision checklist; tick off after reading the matching component notes.</p>
<ul><li>[ ] Exception vs Error — Throwable hierarchy; when default handler prints stack and terminates</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Checked vs unchecked — compiler forces handling for checked; RuntimeException / Error unchecked</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Fully vs partially checked — e.g. Exception / Throwable partially checked; IOException fully checked</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] try-catch control flow — matching catch, no match → propagate; no exception → skip catch</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Multiple catch — child before parent; “exception already caught” CE if order wrong</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] finally — cleanup; executes whether exception raised/handled or not (except System.exit)</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] final vs finally vs finalize — modifier vs block vs Object method (GC last wish)</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] try / catch / finally combinations — illegal standalone catch/finally; try needs catch and/or finally</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] throw — throw new …; throwable object required; after throw, statements unreachable</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] throws — mainly for checked; caller must handle or declare; overriding throws rules</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Custom exceptions — extend Exception (checked) or RuntimeException (unchecked)</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Top exam exceptions — NullPointerException, ClassCastException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, StackOverflowError, etc.</li></ul>
<ul><li>[ ] Java 7+ — try-with-resources (AutoCloseable); multi-catch | syntax</li></ul>
<h2>Exam-checklist snippets (not board timestamps from this mega video)</h2>
<p>The following ``java blocks summarize **key OCJP points** from the component Exception Handling lectures (070–079). They are **exam-checklist snippets**, not claimed board timestamps or a transcript of video VHi9PedZCq8`.</p>
<h3>1) Checked — must catch or declare</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws FileNotFoundException {
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(&quot;abc.txt&quot;);
        pw.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// Without throws / try-catch:
// CE: unreported exception java.io.FileNotFoundException;
//     must be caught or declared to be thrown</pre>
<h3>2) Unchecked — ArithmeticException needs no declare</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(10 / 0); // Runtime: ArithmeticException (unchecked)
    }
}</pre>
<h3>3) try-catch — handled path</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(10 / 0);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;handled&quot;); // prints handled
        }
        System.out.println(&quot;after&quot;); // prints after
    }
}</pre>
<h3>4) Multiple catch — order matters</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(10 / 0);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;AE&quot;); // prints AE
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;E&quot;);
        }
        // catch (Exception e) { }
        // catch (ArithmeticException e) { }
        // CE: exception java.lang.ArithmeticException has already been caught
    }
}</pre>
<h3>5) finally — always (normal case)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(&quot;try&quot;);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;catch&quot;);
        } finally {
            System.out.println(&quot;finally&quot;); // prints finally
        }
        // prints try
        // prints finally
    }
}</pre>
<h3>6) final vs finally vs finalize (exam trio)</h3>
<pre class='code'>final class P { }
// class C extends P { } // CE: cannot inherit from final class P

class Test {
    final int x = 10;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // new Test().x = 20; // CE: cannot assign a value to final variable x
        try {
            // risky
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // handling
        } finally {
            // cleanup associated with try
        }
    }
    public void finalize() {
        // cleanup before GC destroys object (rarely relied on in modern code)
    }
}</pre>
<h3>7) Illegal combination — catch without try</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Bad {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // catch (Exception e) { } // CE: &#x27;catch&#x27; without &#x27;try&#x27;
        // finally { }             // CE: &#x27;finally&#x27; without &#x27;try&#x27;
        try {
            System.out.println(&quot;ok&quot;);
        } finally {
            System.out.println(&quot;try-finally is valid&quot;); // prints try-finally is valid
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>8) throw — explicit throwable</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // throw new Test(); // CE: incompatible types: Test cannot be converted to Throwable
        throw new RuntimeException(&quot;boom&quot;);
        // System.out.println(&quot;hi&quot;); // CE: unreachable statement
    }
}</pre>
<h3>9) throws — checked propagation</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;
class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws IOException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws IOException {
        throw new IOException(&quot;checked&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>10) Custom checked exception</h3>
<pre class='code'>class TooYoungException extends Exception {
    TooYoungException(String s) {
        super(s);
    }
}
class Demo {
    static void register(int age) throws TooYoungException {
        if (age &lt; 18) {
            throw new TooYoungException(&quot;not eligible&quot;);
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            register(16);
        } catch (TooYoungException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage()); // prints not eligible
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>11) Multi-catch (Java 7+)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // risky that may throw AE or NPE
            String s = null;
            System.out.println(s.length());
        } catch (ArithmeticException | NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println(&quot;multi-catch&quot;); // prints multi-catch
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>12) try-with-resources (Java 7+)</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;abc.txt&quot;))) {
            System.out.println(br.readLine());
        } // br.close() called automatically
        // Resource type must implement AutoCloseable / Closeable
    }
}</pre>
